Stick with SAVOL2 for now, though I'd bet if a large survey was done there
would be better algorithms out there.  Here's the gist of what I did:
 

1) The original Still approximation for SASA is not generalizable, and
maybe only useful for very approximate, high through-put calcs.

2) There are several points about SAVOL2:
  a.  differences in SASA are pretty good, but absolute values are high
  b.  maybe changing radii of aliphatic and aromatic carbons, and
carbonyls will improve absolute results
  c.  in 2% of my small molecule test cases, small changes in structure
will give huge changes in SASA --> I don't know from where this stems.
Error was relatively small on large molecules
  d.  it was twice as fast as exact procedures for proteins

3) Exact geometrical procedures, particularly those developed with
peptides in mind, will occasionally fail for numerical reasons.  These
problems are documented in the literature and correctable.


Conclusion:  Still's code is not acceptable for LR type calcs, SAVOL2
appears acceptable with above caveats (perhaps frequent averaging will
drown out occasional large changes), exact procedures would be ideal if
reliable code was obtained.


